Include <strings.h> for strcasecmp, under the macro upstream already tests

`zip_close.c:797` calls `strcasecmp`, and nothing in libzip includes a header
that declares it. That works on glibc and on FreeBSD, where `<string.h>` also
declares the `strcasecmp` pair as an extension - but POSIX puts them in
`<strings.h>`, and a libc that follows POSIX exactly, as `oops-sdk`'s does,
leaves the call undeclared. Under clang 21 that is an error, not a warning.

`compat.h` is where libzip already answers this class of question - it is the
file that substitutes `_stricmp` for `strcasecmp` on MSVC eleven lines further
down - and `HAVE_STRINGS_H` is already computed by upstream's own
`config.h.in`. So this adds the include beside the `<stdbool.h>` one under the
macro that was already there for it, and every platform whose configure step
answers no is untouched.

diff --git a/lib/compat.h b/lib/compat.h
index 49d3aa3..0c772e1 100644
--- a/lib/compat.h
+++ b/lib/compat.h
@@ -62,6 +62,11 @@ typedef char bool;
 #define false 0
 #endif
 
+/* for strcasecmp(), which POSIX declares here rather than in <string.h> */
+#ifdef HAVE_STRINGS_H
+#include <strings.h>
+#endif
+
 #include <errno.h>
 
 /* at least MinGW does not provide EOPNOTSUPP, see
